**Questions/Question 4: The notation of progressive max $max_{l=1}^k$ is somewhat strange and confusing to me, what operation(s) are taken place there? I roughly guess it's something like a "cumulative max", but is it exactly? I would advise the authors to clarity for certain, and try to use clearer notations.**

**Response**: In the context of our paper, progressive max in Equation (5) does not represent cumulative max. Rather, it captures the maximum progress value among the $k$ i.i.d. trials, where each trial represents a complete user-agent multi-turn interaction and the progress value represents the proportion of subgoals achieved at the final conversational turn, with a value close to 1 indicating near completion of all subgoals. The max operation reflects the agent’s best performance in terms of the highest progress achieved across the $k$ trials. To improve clarity, we rewrite the mathematical notation to use the set operator for the max operation and also update the notation $progress_l(i, G_i)$ to  $progress(i,G_i ,\tau_i^l)$. We also provide additional information to clarify all relevant notations in the revised paper.
For reviewer’s convenience, we provide the updated Section 3.2.1 on LLM-as–judge and $MaxProgressRate@k$,  starting from equation (2) to equation (5) below. The revised paper will also include these changes:

The updated section for equation (2):
>*For a task sample $(i, G_i)$, the progress of the agent given its trajectory $\tau_i$, is defined as the proportion of subgoals achieved:*
\begin{equation}
progress(i, G_i, \tau_i)=\frac{1}{|G_i|} \sum_{j=1}^{|G_i|} LLM_{judge}(i, g_{i, j}, \tau_i), \\ \\ \\ \\  \\ \\ \\ \\  \\ \\ \\ \\  \\ \\ \\ \\  (2)
\end{equation}
*where $LLM_{judge}(\cdot)$ returns 1 if the subgoal $g_{i, j}$ is achieved, and 0 otherwise. We define the progress rate as the average progress across all samples in the dataset $D$, i.e., $progressrate = \mathbb{E}_{(i, G_i) \sim P_D} \left[  \ progress(i, G_i, \tau_i) \ \right]
$.*

The updated section for equations (3)-(5):
>*From **$pass@k$ to $MaxProgressRate@k$.**    Given the non-deterministic nature of agent behavior, a commonly used evaluation metric is $pass@k=\mathbb{E}_{Ptask}\left[1- \binom{n-c}{k}/\binom{n}{k}\right]$ (Yao et al., 2024), which measures the probability that at least one trial succeeds  when sampling $k$ out of $n$ total trials. The notation $c$ denotes the number of trials that are successful. Each trial represents a complete multi-turn conversation, consisting of multiple back-and-forth user-agent exchanges. By this definition, when $n=k$, the $pass@k$ metric evaluates to 1 if at least one of the $k$ trials for a given task is successful, and 0 otherwise. The metric then corresponds to the expected maximum success per task, averaged over all tasks, measuring the agent’s best performance across the trials:* 
\begin{equation}
pass@k= \mathbb{E}_{(i, G_i) \sim P_D} \left[ \max  \\{success(i, G_i, \tau_i^l)  \mid l=1, \dots, k \\} \right], \ \mathrm{where} \ \  success(\cdot) \in \\{0,1 \\}. \\ \\  \\ \\ \\ \\ \\  (3)
\end{equation}
*The notation $success(i, G_i, \tau_i^l)$ for a given sample $(i, G_i)$ represents whether the agent with trajectory $\tau_i^l$ successfully completes the task on the $l$-th trial, with a value of 1 for success and 0 for failure. By taking the maximum success over $k$ trials via the $\max\\{ \cdot \\}$ operator, we capture the agent's best performance across these trials. We then relax the strict success condition in  equation 3 by defining a thresholded progress-based success criterion:*

>\begin{equation} 
pass@k= \mathbb{E}_{(i, G_i) \sim P_D} \left[   \max \left\\{ \mathbb{1}\\{  progress(i, G_i, \tau_i^l) \geq threshold  \\} \mid l=1, \dots, k \right\\} \right], \\ \\  \\ \\ \\ \\ \\  (4)
\end{equation}
*where $\mathbb{1}\\{ \cdot \\}$ is the indicator function and the  $threshold \in [0, 1]$ defines the minimum progress for a trial to be considered successful. Setting $threshold=1$ counts only trials with full subgoals completion (i.e., $progress(i, G_i, \tau_i^l)=1$) as successful, and treats any partial progress (i.e., $progress(i, G_i, \tau_i^l) < 1$) as failure.* 

>*Nonetheless, equation 4  applies a hard threshold—treating all progress below the threshold as failure—and discards agent's fine-grained progress. To retain this information, we define a soft version, $MaxProgressRate@k$ to evaluate agent’s best performance based on the maximum progress achieved at the final conversational turn, across $k$ trials, averaged over all samples:*
\begin{equation}
MaxProgressRate@k = \mathbb{E}_{(i, G_i) \sim P_D} \left[ \max \left\\{ progress(i, G_i, \tau_i^l) \mid l=1, \dots, k \right\\} \right]. \\ \\  \\ \\ \\ \\ \\  (5)
\end{equation}
Note that the specific changes for Section 3.2.1 are also annotated in the revised paper.
